 "flat_eye": {
  "pixel": "Kenney tiny-town and tiny-dungeon pictures and three drawn pictures beside our pixel kind's own pack: the drawn bush, ruby and cypress come down to 16 texels a metre with hard edges, the uniform dark-brown Kenney outline takes each thing's own deep shade (green on a bush, purple on the wizard) as the kind draws it, and colours keep to the look's ramp beside each picture's own few (a blue gem stays blue). Still off: Kenney's flat two-tone shading against our dithered shading (the pass adds no shading).",
  "drawn": "Kenney background elements, a toon character, our code-drawn stand-ins and two pixel pictures beside the new-platformer pack: unoutlined pictures get the pack's dark ink edge, colours move toward its bold flat palette. Still off: the pixel pictures stay pixel art (no upscaling into vector), and the background elements keep their soft gradients."
 },
 "findings": [
  "Colour and material levers do not move the artillery case's look: the off-style pieces differ in shape language and surface detail (faceted flat colour against painted rounded art), which the pass never changes by design. The art ladder (library swap, then generation) is the lever there.",
  "Frame-measured colours are darker and greyer than surface colours (light, shade, haze): the pass therefore turns hues and moves only part of the chroma and lightness gap.",
  "Colour alone cannot tell a teal canopy (should be green) from a sea-coloured prop: the asset's own words (slot id, material and mesh names, tags) meet the look colours' roles (MATTER groups). An asset with no words falls back to colour.",
  "Accents need the look's accent colours (ui.accent, vfx.palette) as anchors, else a red barrel drifts toward the nearest earth colour."
 ]
}
